WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:02.059
- [Narrator] This is Buda,

00:00:02.059 --> 00:00:04.190
(whooshing)
and this is Pest.

00:00:04.190 --> 00:00:06.153
Together it makes Budapest.

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:10.070
This is the River Danube,
this is some paprika,

00:00:10.070 --> 00:00:11.910
this is a huge Parliament building,

00:00:11.910 --> 00:00:13.850
and this is some paprika.

00:00:13.850 --> 00:00:15.670
They have a delicious selection of fruits,

00:00:15.670 --> 00:00:17.540
and oh my God, there's paprika.

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:19.580
Pa-pri-ka, paprika!
(popping)

00:00:19.580 --> 00:00:20.903
I mean, it's everywhere.

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:25.153
This has to be a thing.
(popping)

00:00:26.486 --> 00:00:28.470
(calm jazz music)

00:00:28.470 --> 00:00:30.060
This is a paprika pepper.

00:00:30.060 --> 00:00:34.250
Once it's cut, dried, and
grinded down, it makes this:

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:35.850
paprika powder.
(ethereal chimes)

00:00:35.850 --> 00:00:38.420
And here in Hungary, it's a big deal.

00:00:38.420 --> 00:00:40.520
Known to locals as the Red Gold,

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:44.300
this spicy ingredient is
in pretty much everything.

00:00:44.300 --> 00:00:48.430
Hungarian goulash, chicken
paprika, stuffed cabbage.

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:51.930
I mean, in some places,
it's even next to the salt.

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:54.000
Here at the Great Market Hall of Budapest,

00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:55.510
well, you guessed it.

00:00:55.510 --> 00:00:58.880
- We have a huge range of paprika
from all over the country.

00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:01.600
We use it for almost every Hungarian food,

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:03.250
and it's very delicious.

00:01:03.250 --> 00:01:06.960
We even have a museum and a
festival dedicated to paprika.

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:07.793
- [Narrator] Really?

00:01:07.793 --> 00:01:09.090
- Yes.
(whooshing)

00:01:09.090 --> 00:01:11.470
- Welcome to PaprikaMolnár Museum.

00:01:11.470 --> 00:01:15.407
My name is Anita Molnar, and
I am the paprika historian.

00:01:15.407 --> 00:01:17.590
(popping)
Paprika is a very common

00:01:17.590 --> 00:01:20.880
Hungarian spice that was created

00:01:20.880 --> 00:01:24.600
about 200 years ago in
this region, in Szeged.

00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:28.280
It is a spice; it colors your food red;

00:01:28.280 --> 00:01:32.913
and we Hungarians tend to maybe
overuse it, but we love it.

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:37.660
Originally, it was brought
over to Europe by Columbus.

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:40.410
Therefore, the mother plant was definitely

00:01:40.410 --> 00:01:42.100
some kind of a chili.
(popping)

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:44.150
People who were trying it, they figured

00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:46.140
it was a lot like black pepper.

00:01:46.140 --> 00:01:48.490
For people who couldn't afford to buy

00:01:48.490 --> 00:01:51.570
very expensive black
pepper, they started to use

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:55.167
the fruit of this plant
as a pepper substitute.

00:01:55.167 --> 00:01:57.230
(plucky string music)

00:01:57.230 --> 00:01:59.550
- [Narrator] But nowadays,
it's not just used for food.

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:01.018
Isn't that right, Peter?

00:02:01.018 --> 00:02:04.500
- Yes, definitely we
have some paprika soap.

00:02:04.500 --> 00:02:07.860
You know, the soap has
some paprika aromas.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.670
It doesn't hurt when you
wash yourself. (laughs)

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:11.560
- [Narrator] But that's not all.

00:02:11.560 --> 00:02:12.777
They've got paprika-flavored honey,

00:02:12.777 --> 00:02:14.000
(popping)
paprika-flavored palinka,

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:15.956
paprika-flavored
chocolate. (catches breath)

00:02:15.956 --> 00:02:17.530
- Everything paprika.

00:02:17.530 --> 00:02:19.110
- [Narrator] Okay, back to the kitchen.

00:02:19.110 --> 00:02:21.360
This stuff may be ancient, but it's on

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:23.583
some of the city's most modern menus.

00:02:51.197 --> 00:02:52.918
- [Narrator] Let's try this.

00:02:52.918 --> 00:02:54.950
Mm, oh my God.

00:02:54.950 --> 00:02:55.783
So good.

00:02:55.783 --> 00:02:58.790
(plucky string music)

00:02:58.790 --> 00:03:02.457
(high-pitched vibrato note)

